  "facts": [],
  "plan": {
    "decision": "write",
    "reason": "The PR adds a priority filter to the list endpoint. The repository `where` branch (repository.ts:9) and the router 422 branch (router.ts:15) are uncovered, and no test checks that a parsed priority reaches the repository or that an invalid one is rejected. Backend-only cases are planned; the remaining gaps are listed separately.",
    "cases": [
      {
        "id": "repo-list-priority-where",
        "sourceLocation": "backend/src/features/todos/repository.ts:9",
        "behavior": "list({priority}) restricts the Prisma query to that priority and keeps newest-first ordering; list() and list({}) add no where clause.",
        "requirementSource": "openapi.json GET /api/todos 'priority' query parameter ('Filter tasks by priority.'); TodoStore.list(query?: TodoListQuery) in types.ts",
        "inputs": "repository.list({ priority: 'low' }) with findMany mocked to return [record]; then repository.list({}) in the same test.",
        "expected": "First findMany call receives { where: { priority: 'low' }, orderBy: [{ created_at: 'desc' }, { id: 'desc' }] }. Second call has no 'where' key. Result is mapped via fromModel.",
        "assertion": "expect(findMany).toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(1, { where: { priority: 'low' }, orderBy: [...] }); expect(findMany.mock.calls[1][0]).not.toHaveProperty('where'); expect(result).toEqual([todo]).",
        "mockBoundary": "vi.spyOn on the real Prisma delegate's findMany, as in the existing tests; no database.",
        "confidence": "contract-backed",
        "value": {
          "action": "add",
          "testPath": "backend/tests/repository.test.ts",
          "existingTests": [
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/repository.test.ts",
              "name": "lists tasks in newest-first order and maps database rows"
            }
          ],
          "regression": "The filter is dropped, so every task is returned. Or the filter is applied to the wrong column. Or the empty query adds where: { priority: undefined } instead of omitting it.",
          "rationale": "The existing test calls list() only, so the new where branch is untested. The filter is the PR's main behavior and needs one cheap spy-based case."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "router-list-forwards-priority",
        "sourceLocation": "backend/src/features/todos/router.ts:15",
        "behavior": "GET /api/todos?priority=high parses the query and passes { priority: 'high' } to repository.list.",
        "requirementSource": "openapi.json GET /api/todos 'priority' query parameter with enum low|medium|high",
        "inputs": "GET /api/todos?priority=high",
        "expected": "Status 200, body [todo], repository.list called once with { priority: 'high' }.",
        "assertion": "expect(response.status).toBe(200); expect(repository.list).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ priority: 'high' }); expect(response.body).toEqual([todo]).",
        "mockBoundary": "Fake TodoStore passed to createTodosRouter; supertest over an in-memory express app.",
        "confidence": "contract-backed",
        "value": {
          "action": "add",
          "testPath": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
          "existingTests": [
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
              "name": "lists tasks from the repository"
            }
          ],
          "regression": "The router ignores req.query and calls repository.list() with no filter, so clients always get every task.",
          "rationale": "The existing test only checks list was called once and cannot tell whether a priority was forwarded. The case reuses the existing setup."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "router-list-rejects-invalid-priority",
        "sourceLocation": "backend/src/features/todos/router.ts:15",
        "behavior": "An out-of-enum priority query returns 422 with validation issues and never reaches the repository.",
        "requirementSource": "openapi.json GET /api/todos response '422' (Invalid input) and the priority enum",
        "inputs": "GET /api/todos?priority=urgent",
        "expected": "Status 422; body.detail is a non-empty array; repository.list is not called.",
        "assertion": "expect(response.status).toBe(422); expect(Array.isArray(response.body.detail)).toBe(true); expect(response.body.detail.length).toBeGreaterThan(0); expect(repository.list).not.toHaveBeenCalled().",
        "mockBoundary": "Fake TodoStore and supertest, as in the existing router tests.",
        "confidence": "contract-backed",
        "value": {
          "action": "add",
          "testPath": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
          "existingTests": [
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
              "name": "lists tasks from the repository"
            },
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
              "name": "rejects invalid patch body without updating: %j"
            }
          ],
          "regression": "An invalid priority is silently ignored or passed through to the repository, giving a 200 or a Prisma error instead of the documented 422.",
          "rationale": "router.ts:15 is an uncovered branch and the documented 422 response is new in this PR. The test follows the existing invalid-input pattern at low cost."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "router-list-no-query-forwards-empty",
        "sourceLocation": "backend/src/features/todos/router.ts:15",
        "behavior": "With no priority, and with unrelated query keys, the router passes an object with no priority key to repository.list.",
        "requirementSource": "listQuerySchema in validators.ts makes priority optional; z.object strips unknown keys (characterization of the zod default, not a documented guarantee)",
        "inputs": "GET /api/todos?unrelated=1",
        "expected": "Status 200; repository.list called with {}.",
        "assertion": "expect(repository.list).toHaveBeenCalledWith({}) — a priority key with value undefined is not distinguishable by toHaveBeenCalledWith, so the case also checks Object.keys(repository.list.mock.calls[0][0]) is empty.",
        "mockBoundary": "Fake TodoStore and supertest.",
        "confidence": "characterization",
        "value": {
          "action": "strengthen",
          "testPath": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
          "existingTests": [
            {
              "path": "backend/tests/router.test.ts",
              "name": "lists tasks from the repository"
            }
          ],
          "regression": "Raw req.query is forwarded, so unrelated parameters leak to the repository. Or an unfiltered request becomes a filter with an invalid value.",
          "rationale": "The existing assertion, calledOnce, ignores the argument. Adding the argument check to the existing scenario costs one line."
        }
      }
    ],
    "paths": [
      "backend/tests/repository.test.ts",
      "backend/tests/router.test.ts"
    ],
    "remainingGaps": [
      "No test checks the openapi.json priority parameter or 422 response. factory.test.ts stubs the spec, so asserting document content there would need a real-file read and is not planned.",
      "Repeated priority parameters (?priority=a&priority=b) produce an array under Express query parsing; the intended behavior is not specified, so no case is planned.",
      "Frontend does not use the priority filter yet; no frontend cases are justified by this PR.",
      "Pre-existing uncovered locations outside this PR (backend/src/main.ts, factory.ts:20, frontend AppProviders and others) were not analyzed."
    ]
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "passed",
    "reason": "The tests pass review. All four planned cases run the real unit (the real TodoRepository on a spied Prisma delegate, and the real router with zod validation through supertest). Each planned regression fails at least one assertion. Repository case: dropping the filter, filtering on the wrong column, or adding `where: { priority: undefined }` each fail, through toHaveBeenNthCalledWith and `not.toHaveProperty('where')`. The `not.toHaveProperty('where')` check catches what toEqual would miss, because toEqual ignores keys whose value is undefined. Router forwarding case: calling `list()` with no filter fails `toHaveBeenCalledWith({ priority: 'high' })`. Invalid-priority case: ignoring or passing through `urgent` produces a 200 and a repository call, which fails the 422 and `not.toHaveBeenCalled` assertions. No-query case: forwarding raw `req.query` for `?unrelated=1` gives `['unrelated']`, which fails the `Object.keys(...)` check against `[]`. Forwarding `{ priority: undefined }` or `undefined` also fails `Object.keys`. The stripping behaviour is honestly labelled as a characterization. The setup reuses existing fixtures, and the new cases protect different things rather than repeating existing assertions. All required backend and frontend checks passed.",
    "findings": [
      {
        "id": "router-redundant-empty-assert",
        "severity": "minor",
        "location": "backend/tests/router.test.ts 'lists tasks from the repository'",
        "claim": "`toHaveBeenCalledWith({})` adds nothing that the following `Object.keys(...).toEqual([])` assertion does not already cover.",
        "evidence": "toHaveBeenCalledWith uses recursive equality, so it accepts `{ priority: undefined }`. The next line, `Object.keys(repository.list.mock.calls[0][0])` equal to `[]`, rejects both that and any extra keys. So every mutant caught by the first assertion is also caught by the second. This is cheap, harmless duplication.",
        "kind": "other",
        "factId": null
      }
    ]
  },
